{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdZ88nTyQjBAyMcoU3e4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Santiago Schnell's description of his AI use shifted over several weeks, and the faculty committee that reviewed it concluded that he \"was not forthcoming about his AI use.\" Schnell, Dartmouth's provost, resigned Wednesday. President Sian Leah Beilock said the decision was his.\n\nIt started when Semafor ran guest columns in major newspapers through an AI filter. Schnell's August Washington Post column on AI cheating was found to be entirely written by AI. He said at the time that he used ChatGPT to \"refine a few arguments\" and that the column came from his own thinking. Those two accounts differ a great deal in scale, and the difference prompted the student paper, The Dartmouth, to run his other publications through a detector. It reported that writing from before ChatGPT's 2022 release read as human-written and that his recent writing showed high rates of AI use. When Schnell emailed the paper that he uses AI for \"language refinement, copyediting, improving clarity and organization,\" the paper's detector flagged that email as AI-written as well. He then said he had developed the substance and written a draft, and used an AI tool to assist with language and clarity.\n\nThe detector findings came from a news outlet and a student paper, and the article does not say how accurate those tools are, so they should be read as reported results and not as established fact. The committee's conclusion is a separate and more formal one: that he used AI to \"meaningfully alter his writing.\" That wording is narrower than \"entirely written by AI,\" and the article does not describe what evidence or method the committee relied on. Schnell has referred questions to his attorneys, who had not responded to the AP.\n\nBeilock's statement tied the outcome to transparency, saying leaders should model \"the transparency and human judgment that we expect from our students and scholars.\" Read alongside the sequence of events, this suggests that what ended his tenure was the distance between how he described his AI use and what reviewers concluded about his writing. That is an inference from the article, which does not rank the committee's two findings. It carries extra weight here because the column in question argued about how universities should respond to AI cheating, at a time when colleges are rethinking teaching as more assignments can be completed with AI tools."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1878,"cache_read_input_tokens":2544,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1878},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92b14bfc-b269-494b-a37d-3c81f9d46641","uuid":"6c17a9ec-e4e5-4b5a-bd64-503867c8c693","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:32:43.818Z","request_id":"req_011CgdZ88YKuqMic6KaV3Kap"}
